SETUID(2)                      P32W Unix                       SETUID(2)

NAME
       setuid, setgid - set user and group ID

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       int setuid(uid_t uid);
       int setgid(gid_t gid);

DESCRIPTION
       setuid sets both the real and the effective user ID of the
       calling process to uid. The super-user (effective user ID 0)
       may set any ID; anyone else only uid equal to the current
       real user ID, which lets a set-user-ID program drop back to
       its user's identity.

       setgid does the same for the real and effective group IDs.
       Its permission check too is on the effective user ID: the
       super-user may set any group, others only their real group
       ID.

       login(1) and su(1) call setgid and then setuid.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is not the super-user and the ID is not
               its real ID.

SEE ALSO
       getuid(2), exec(2), login(1), su(1)

BUGS
       There is no seteuid, setreuid or saved set-user-ID: a
       set-user-ID program that gives up its privilege cannot get
       it back. There are no supplementary groups.
